WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:02.440
Today curator Katy Barrett shares how

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.820
one of the most famous British artists of all time

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.900
took her sketchbook into surgery,

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.680
in another story from the stores.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:12.320
Why would a famous artist be sat in

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.520
an operating theatre sketching?

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.240
Barbara Hepworth's daughter was very ill in the 1940s.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.440
She had a bone infection disease,

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.060
and was sent to a surgeon called Norman Capener,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.840
and Norman Capener invited Barbara Hepworth

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:27.080
to go and draw in surgery because he

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.960
felt she must have an affinity with

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.360
looking at human body.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.560
This sketchbook comes from a series of operations

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.580
that she saw at the London clinic that

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.820
were to do in an operation called fenestration of the ear.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.500
And can we see the sketches?

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.620
Absolutely we can. This is amazing because it's the

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.460
only surviving sketchbook from all of

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.540
the observations that Hepworth did.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:48.720
Some of them are very simple,

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.440
you can just see the head of the surgeon here,

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.400
he's looking through the equipment,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.760
then you get on to other kinds of annotation,

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:57.620
so she's marking the equipment that's being used,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.600
later on she marks colours.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.200
She's doing these incredibly quickly,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.059
obviously she's not allowed to change what's happening

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:04.439
in the surgery, she can't make them stop

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.320
when they're in a nice composition,

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.020
so she's recording what she thinks she

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:10.800
might want to draw later.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.440
What makes it stand out as one of her pieces?

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.080
Ah okay, so it's interesting.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.210
Actually this period of work is very unusual in

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.420
Barbara Hepworth's work as a whole because

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:22.400
she didn't tend to do figure studies.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.980
She tended to be very abstract, but she wrote

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.790
quite a lot about these observations

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.680
that she made in the surgery.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:30.680
This is actually the sketch which became

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.080
the work in the Tate, and we can see how

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.400
she's got this real interest in hands and faces.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.700
You can see the hands and faces and the tools.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.740
She was particularly interested in what she called

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:44.320
the harmony and beauty of how surgery worked together,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.620
and she saw it as naturally making the kind of

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.380
composition that she was trying to make in her sculpture.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.000
Also the process of doing an operation like this,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.420
fenestration actually involves hammering

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.580
a bit of bone out of the skull,

00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.400
uses a hammer and chisel, which are the kind of

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:58.820
tools she of course was using in sculpture.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.320
So she was particularly interested in

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.300
those kind of parities between surgery and art.

